<section>
  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    Using <i>m</i> = 80 kg and <i>g</i> = 9.81 m/s², first with the lift at
    rest (<i>a</i> = 0):
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><i>T</i> = 80 × 9.81 = 784.8 N, the true weight</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Now take the default <i>a</i> = 1.2 m/s² upward:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><i>T</i> − 784.8 = 80 × 1.2</li>
    <li><i>T</i> = 784.8 + 96 = 880.8 N</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    These match the live values. Reverse the acceleration to
    <i>a</i> = −1.2 m/s² (accelerating down):
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><i>T</i> = 80(9.81 − 1.2) = 688.8 N</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The scale reading rises when the lift accelerates up and falls when it
    accelerates down. At <i>a</i> = −9.81 m/s² the status becomes SLACK and
    <i>T</i> = 0.
  </p>
</section>
